{"step_index":7564,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-25T17:13:30Z","tool_calls":[{"name":"search_web","args":{"query":"\"\\\"az restore-point collection create\\\" site:learn.microsoft.com\"","toolAction":"\"Searching exact command syntax\"","toolSummary":"\"Search az restore-point collection create syntax\""}}]}
{"step_index":7565,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-25T17:13:33Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-25T17:13:33Z\nCompleted At: 2026-09-25T17:13:37Z\nThe search for \"\"az restore-point collection create\" site:learn.microsoft.com\" returned the following summary:\nThe `az restore-point collection create` command is used in the Azure CLI to create or update a restore point collection for virtual machines[1][2].\n\n### Key Details\n*   **Purpose:** This command initializes a restore point collection, which is a required container for creating individual VM restore points[1][2].\n*   **Updating:** When using this command to update an existing collection, note that **only tags** may be modified[3][1].\n*   **Key Parameters:**\n    *   `--location`: The region where the restore point collection will be stored[2].\n    *   `--source-id`: The resource ID of the virtual machine you are protecting[2].\n    *   `--instant-access`: A parameter that enables instant access, allowing for faster disk restoration (applicable to specific disk types like Premium SSD v2 or Ultra disks)[4][5].\n\n### Example Usage\nTo create a restore point collection, you typically run a command similar to the following:\n\n```bash\naz restore-point collection create \\\n  --location \"eastus\" \\\n  --resource-group \"MyResourceGroup\" \\\n  --collection-name \"MyRestorePointCollection\" \\\n  --source-id \"/subscriptions/{sub-id}/resourceGroups/{rg}/providers/Microsoft.Compute/virtualMachines/{vm-name}\"\n```\n\nFor more detailed information and the full list of supported arguments, you can refer to the official [Microsoft Learn documentation for `az restore-point collection`](https://learn.microsoft.com).\n\nSources:\n[1] [microsoft.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQG4w9G3i66qgyuzgH8etDd31HhjqEcoUriFNgJbm2HTz39cZ8adVaY03-a97701emj6CpvkZPeThnh0-1--5trNAgwTvwzFx4J6459p5acpfPtb_KOiyzmiXzkrWr6j7l4D-zkh9zT5FmcQoe6PThH5BnOQqsbRGVM4pcxGvZujiA5aROg=)\n[2] [microsoft.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFCtflz2BzpxD9MjaW-hKBOicPrgDdBtF7QSOzqy2TgC-Gi2q7b5E_Jw6vlQ435X8h8CIXp-Ix6uKe-qdO0JeMCLJchL19Svk66V6NOCpF4_SlQ0qIV9Nh0xvxLwdVkSLLtxcC7EI-dE--DDLbs8lUtrIee7TLTSTCQCbAX-OV4Abt-gi1SqQlaq00ZEGVBDVER0U_R7oj8Ig==)\n[3] [microsoft.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGON16oygkxYV5aN8Q3OHBN5v4X7ai-SaSo_XizDu0DJPk2AKrQh8wVwALKTR2tnPDFVcWyHEiVu-xRGvntZE0P0WEf5qs_ohLsMtq7kpGR0GEieaTkwNmQYAI0R_aZ4ukB9iRm1oveuQ38vdgT__fMPi_DcWw7mm59pmhIraZIs6W8YHebX9mGGYzSIjkt7Q==)\n[4] [microsoft.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQF2op8EdMw0JgXWIlB5Wk_vrYlTJ_Q8lo5KjHskzX0QkAfWLEFY5seK1wUhDYdnwBwm5oBJxNNxwrYLskSvYscCAU0KiSkMza3QxeQv6pML_wzOz8TLhI8hsPZYsByJqw1V8pQ1QrB8anYnqM3sHOjN_7b-vI55aa97CZZpAPW2Xg9-YsOn6K4AdiHuYbM2)\n[5] [microsoft.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFCtflz2BzpxD9MjaW-hKBOicPrgDdBtF7QSOzqy2TgC-Gi2q7b5E_Jw6vlQ435X8h8CIXp-Ix6uKe-qdO0JeMCLJchL19Svk66V6NOCpF4_SlQ0qIV9Nh0xvxLwdVkSLLtxcC7EI-dE--DDLbs8lUtrIee7TLTSTCQCbAX-OV4Abt-gi1SqQlaq00ZEGVBDVER0U_R7oj8Ig==)"}
{"step_index":7566,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-25T17:13:37Z","tool_calls":[{"name":"search_web","args":{"query":"\"\\\"az restore-point create\\\" site:learn.microsoft.com\"","toolAction":"\"Searching az restore-point create syntax\"","toolSummary":"\"Search az restore-point create parameters\""}}]}
{"step_index":7567,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-25T17:13:42Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-25T17:13:42Z\nCompleted At: 2026-09-25T17:13:44Z\nThe search for \"\"az restore-point create\" site:learn.microsoft.com\" returned the following summary:\nThe `az restore-point create` command in the Azure CLI is used to create a restore point for a virtual machine within an existing restore point collection[1][2].\n\n### Basic Usage\nTo create a restore point, you must specify the resource group, the name of the restore point collection, and the name for the new restore point[1]:\n\n```bash\naz restore-point create \\\n    --resource-group \"ExampleRg\" \\\n    --collection-name \"ExampleRpc\" \\\n    --name \"ExampleRp\"\n```\n\n### Key Considerations\n*   **Restore Point Collections:** Before creating a restore point, you must first create a \"restore point collection\" using `az restore-point collection create`[1][2].\n*   **Consistency Modes:** You can optionally set the `--consistency-mode` parameter to `CrashConsistent` if you are creating a crash-consistent restore point (currently in preview)[1].\n*   **Instant Access:** If your restore point collection has \"Instant Access\" enabled (supported for specific disk types like Premium SSD v2 or Ultra disks), you can specify the `--instant-access-duration` parameter[1][3].\n*   **Immutability:** Once a restore point is created, its properties cannot be updated[4][5].\n*   **Monitoring Progress:** You can track the status of the creation process using the `az restore-point show` command[6].\n\n### Recent Updates\nRecent updates to the Azure CLI have added support for disk encryption during the restore point creation process, including parameters such as `--os-restore-point-encryption-set`, `--os-restore-point-encryption-type`, `--data-disk-restore-point-encryption-set`, and `--data-disk-restore-point-encryption-type`[7].\n\nSources:\n[1] [microsoft.com](https://vertexaisearch.clo